/****************************************************************************
 * board/contest_board/src/bk7258_reset.c
 *
 * Reset-cause reporting for boardctl(BOARDIOC_RESET_CAUSE), which the xTS
 * general self-test 1.3.15 (Watchdog) reads back after each bite.
 *
 * Where the reason lives
 * ----------------------
 * BK7258 builds as CONFIG_SOC_BK7236XX in the vendor SDK
 * (middleware/soc/bk7258/bk7258.defconfig line 2), so the reset reason
 * follows the BK7236XX/BK7239XX branch of the SDK's reset_reason.c:
 *
 *   read   aon_pmu_ll_get_r7a() >> 24 & 0x7f
 *   write  read-modify-write of PMU R0 bits [30:24], then commit by writing
 *          0x424B55AA and 0xBDB4AA55 to PMU R25
 *
 * The R25 pair is the vendor's latch sequence that copies R0 into the
 * retained R7B/R7A shadow; without it a value written to R0 does not
 * survive the reset.  All three registers sit in the always-on domain
 * (SOC_AON_PMU_REG_BASE == 0x44000000, include/soc/bk7258/reg_base.h:54),
 * which is why the value outlives a watchdog reset at all.
 *
 * Register offsets, from middleware/soc/bk7258/soc/aon_pmu_ll.h:
 *   R0   base + (0x00 << 2) == 0x44000000
 *   R25  base + (0x25 << 2) == 0x44000094
 *   R7A  base + (0x7a << 2) == 0x440001e8
 *
 * When it is safe to touch
 * ------------------------
 * Never from __start().  An AON access issued before the domain is ready
 * stalls the bus on this SoC -- that is what bricked the board twice early
 * in the port (PORTING_NOTES chapter 2).  The latch therefore runs from
 * board_late_initialize(), the same place the AON RTC comes up, and the
 * value is cached because the vendor's own read is documented "only can be
 * do once".
 *
